{
  "assessment": "This change introduces latency/percentiles.py with a nearest-rank percentile calculator, but the index formula int(n * p) consistently overshoots by one rank for standard window sizes. Consequently, value_at returns the maximum observation instead of the specified percentile boundary, violating the documented computation rules.\n\nDefect 1:\n- call: python -c \"from latency.percentiles import value_at; print(value_at(list(range(1, 21)), 0.95))\"\n- expected: 19 (the nineteenth observation in a sorted sequence)\n- actual: 20 (the twentieth observation / maximum)",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "20",
      "at": "latency/percentiles.py: value_at",
      "call": "python -c \"from latency.percentiles import value_at; print(value_at(list(range(1, 21)), 0.95))\"",
      "defect": "index = int(n * p) computes the nineteenth percentile of twenty samples as index 19 (the maximum), whereas the specification requires index 18.",
      "expected": "19"
    }
  ]
}
